      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-143474",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, \u2026, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}\u223cN(\u03b8, 1^{2})$, where $\u03b8$ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, \u2026, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$\u2211_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i} = 30.2, \u2211_(i = 1)^(12)x_{i}^{2} = 90.6$.\nGiven that the log-likelihood function of the sample is\n$l(\u03b8) =-6\\ln (2\u03c0) + a + b\u03b8 + c\u03b8^{2}$,\nwhat is the value of $a + b + c$?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\u03b8$ and standard deviation $1$ is given by*\n$f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\u03c0}}e^{- (x - \u03b8)^{2}/2}, x \u2208 (- \u221e, \u221e)$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    },
